//
// THE FONT — the ONE seam between dryopea and `graphics::draw_text`.
//
// #examples
//
// BACKLOG B1.  ⚠⚠ **dryopea rasterises nothing here.**  `graphics`
// already owns the glyph work (`gl_load_font` / `gl_measure_text` /
// `gl_text_height` / `draw_text`), so what this file owns is the
// three things `graphics` cannot know: **WHICH font, WHERE it lives,
// and what a font that did not load is allowed to do.**
//
// ── The invariant ───────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **dryopea owns the font's ABSOLUTE path and loads it once; every
// draw goes through one door that has already proven the handle
// real — so neither the caller's cwd, nor the entry's directory, nor
// the `graphics` version can decide which typeface appears, and a
// font that did not load REFUSES BY NAME instead of silently drawing
// in somebody else's.**
//
// ── Why that sentence has three clauses ─────────────────────────
//
// Each one is a MEASURED failure, not a precaution (`@M047`).
//
// ⚠⚠ **A NULL HANDLE DRAWS, AND IT DRAWS IN WHATEVER FONT LOADED
// FIRST.**  Measured: with a good font already at handle 0, a failed
// `gl_load_font` returns null, and `gl_measure_text(null, "X", 16)`
// answers **12.34** — the same as the good handle — while
// `draw_text` puts down the same **128** pixels.  A `null` reaching
// the native boundary collapses to **0**, which is a real handle.
// So the failure of a font to load is not *nothing appears*; it is
// *the wrong typeface appears, silently*.  ⚠ With NO font loaded at
// all it does draw nothing (0 pixels) — which is exactly what makes
// the trap invisible in a one-font test and live in a two-font one.
// **That is why `Font` carries `loaded` and every door asks it.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **A RELATIVE PATH MEANS TWO DIFFERENT THINGS, AND WHICH ONE
// DEPENDS ON THE `graphics` VERSION.**  Measured across the two
// releases installed on this box:
//
//   graphics 0.5.2 (PINNED)  `gl_load_font` IS the raw native —
//                            the path resolves against the PROCESS
//                            CWD.  `assets/…` loads; `../assets/…`
//                            does not.
//   graphics 0.8.0           its #255 fix joins `source_dir()` —
//                            the path resolves against the ENTRY's
//                            directory.  `assets/…` would become
//                            `tests/assets/…` and load NOTHING.
//
// So `loft install graphics` would silently stop the font loading,
// and by the paragraph above that is not a blank screen but the
// wrong face.  ⚠ An **absolute** path is the one form both releases
// pass through verbatim, which is why `text_font_path` builds one
// rather than trusting either convention.
//
// ⚠ **`#cwd` does NOT move `source_dir()`** — measured: under
// `loft test` it still answers `<repo>/tests`.  The two mechanisms
// are unrelated, so dryopea's `#cwd` + repo-root-relative rule (which
// governs `file()`, and therefore `examples/palette.json`) says
// nothing about where a font is found.
//
// ── Why the path is built from `source_dir()` ───────────────────
//
// ⚠ It is the only ABSOLUTE anchor pointing into the repo that loft
// offers: there is no `cwd()` builtin, and `program_directory()` is
// the loft binary's.  Every dryopea entry — `src/main.loft`,
// `src/validate_main.loft`, `src/gl_gate_main.loft`,
// `src/mapbuild_main.loft` and every `tests/*.loft` — sits exactly
// ONE level under the repo root, so `{source_dir()}/../assets` is the
// root's `assets/` from all of them.  ⚠⚠ **That is an assumption
// about the tree's SHAPE, so it is not left to a comment**:
// `text_fault` asks `is_file` and NAMES the path it tried, and
// `tests/b1_the_text_seam.loft` fails on the name rather than on a
// blank canvas.  A nested entry directory would go red at once.
//
// ── What this seam does NOT decide ──────────────────────────────
//
// ⚠⚠ **Anti-aliased text is not a palette colour, and the number is
// large** (`@M047`): one 18 px line of white-on-black measures 1324
// lit pixels of which only **550 are pure white** — **774 are
// blended**.  `measure.loft::classify_canvas` looks colours up
// EXACTLY, so every one of those 774 lands in `unknown`, and both
// `@X077`'s `other == 0` and `@X092`'s `unknown - entity pixels == 0`
// are assertions text would break.
//
// ⚠ **That is the first CONSUMER's problem and it is deliberately
// left open here.**  The precedent is already in the tree and it is
// `@M042`'s: the wallet overlay is composed on its OWN canvas and
// blitted 1:1, so the gate counts the ink in that canvas rather than
// hunting it in the frame.  A door that thresholds text to one flat
// colour is the other candidate — cheap to add over `text_draw`, and
// **not added now, because a door with no consumer is a door nobody
// has measured**.  ⚠ What must NOT happen is loosening
// `classify_canvas` to a nearest-colour match: `docs/RENDERER.md`
// § R0 measured exact classification through GL at zero drift, and
// that property is what three gates stand on.

use graphics;

// The font, relative to the repo root.  ⚠ DejaVu Sans **Bold**:
// BOLD because text is drawn over a busy world and a hairline face is
// unreadable there, and DejaVu because its Bitstream Vera licence is
// permissive — `assets/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt` travels with it, as that
// licence requires.  ⚠ Liberation Sans Narrow (which `graphics`
// itself bundles) was refused on licence rather than on looks: it is
// GPL-2-with-a-font-exception, which is copyleft, and this repo is
// LGPL-3.0-or-later.
pub const TEXT_FONT_FILE: text = "assets/DejaVuSans-Bold.ttf";

// A handle that is not a font.  ⚠ NOT 0 — 0 is the first handle
// `gl_load_font` hands out, so 0 is the value a `null` collapses to
// and the one that must never mean *absent*.
pub const FONT_NO_HANDLE: integer = -1;

// A loaded font.
//
// ── Why there are TWO fields for one fact ───────────────────────
//
// ⚠⚠ `loaded` looks redundant beside a negative `handle`, and the
// repo's own rule is against it — `carry.loft` refuses a *carried*
// flag beside an owner because **two facts can disagree**.  It is
// here anyway, and the reason is [loft#914]: **a struct literal that
// omits a field takes that field's default SILENTLY.**  Which
// default a partial literal lands on is therefore the whole
// question, and the two designs land on opposite ones (measured):
//
//   `Font { handle: h, path: p }`   omits `loaded` -> **false**
//                                   -> `font_ready` says no
//                                   -> FAILS CLOSED.
//   readiness as `handle >= 0`      omits `handle` -> **0**
//                                   -> 0 is a LIVE handle
//                                   -> FAILS INTO THE TRAP.
//
// So the second fact is not duplication, it is the direction the
// silent default falls in.  ⚠ The two can only disagree through a
// hand-built literal, because `font_load_from` is the only
// constructor of a loaded `Font` and it sets both at one site.
//
// ⚠⚠ **The doors read `loaded`, never `handle >= 0`** — and the two
// stop DIFFERENT failures, which is measured rather than assumed
// (`tests/b1_the_font_seam.loft`).  Delete the `text_draw` guard and
// a *missing file* still draws nothing, because `FONT_NO_HANDLE` is
// not a handle `graphics` will use: what breaks is `text_draw`'s
// ANSWER, which starts saying `true` while painting nothing.  What
// the guard stops in PIXELS is the other case — a `Font` carrying a
// LIVE handle with `loaded` false, which is what a partial literal
// makes — and that one paints the wrong typeface exactly (203 pixels
// against the good font's 203).
pub struct Font {
    handle: integer,
    path:   text,
    loaded: boolean,
}

// Where the repo's font is, as an ABSOLUTE path.
//
// ⚠⚠ Absolute is the requirement, not a convenience: it is the one
// form `graphics` 0.5.2 and 0.8.0 agree on (§ A RELATIVE PATH MEANS
// TWO DIFFERENT THINGS).  ⚠ It is a function and not a `const`
// because `source_dir()` is `#impure(host_io)` and loft refuses a
// call in a const initialiser.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn text_font_path() -> text {
    "{source_dir()}/../{TEXT_FONT_FILE}"
}

// A `Font` that never loaded — what a caller holds before asking, and
// what `font_load_from` answers on any failure.
//
// Example: @DRY-097
pub fn font_missing() -> Font {
    Font { handle: FONT_NO_HANDLE, path: "", loaded: false }
}

// Load a font from an ABSOLUTE path.  ⚠ The door for a test, or for a
// future setting that names a face; `font_load` is the one the game
// calls.
//
// ⚠ It asks `is_file` FIRST so the two failures stay apart: *the file
// is not there* and *the file is there and the loader refused it* are
// different problems with different fixes, and one message covering
// both would name neither.
//
// Example: @DRY-097
pub fn font_load_from(flf_path: text) -> Font {
    // ⚠⚠ ABSOLUTE is ENFORCED, not just documented.  A relative path
    // would pass `is_file` (cwd-relative) and then mean something
    // ELSE to `gl_load_font` depending on the `graphics` version —
    // which is the whole failure this file exists to prevent, and a
    // public door that accepts one silently is where it would come
    // back in.
    if !flf_path.starts_with("/") {
        return Font { handle: FONT_NO_HANDLE, path: flf_path, loaded: false };
    }
    if !is_file(flf_path) {
        return Font { handle: FONT_NO_HANDLE, path: flf_path, loaded: false };
    }
    flf_h = graphics::gl_load_font(flf_path);
    if flf_h == null or flf_h < 0 {
        return Font { handle: FONT_NO_HANDLE, path: flf_path, loaded: false };
    }
    Font { handle: flf_h, path: flf_path, loaded: true }
}

// Load the repo's own font.  ⚠ The ONE call the game makes, and it
// takes no argument on purpose: a path parameter here is a second
// place the answer to *which font* could live.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn font_load() -> Font {
    font_load_from(text_font_path())
}

// May this font be drawn with?  ⚠⚠ **Every door below asks this, and
// nothing outside this file should have to** — that is what makes the
// seam a chokepoint rather than a convention.
//
// Example: none — a one-line accessor over `f.loaded`
pub fn font_ready(fr_f: const Font) -> boolean {
    fr_f.loaded
}

// What is wrong with this font — `""` when nothing is.
//
// ⚠ It NAMES the path, because the failure this file exists to
// prevent is a silent one: a blank corner and a corner drawn in the
// wrong face look identical, and neither says where it looked.
//
// Example: @DRY-097
pub fn text_fault(tf_f: const Font) -> text {
    if font_ready(tf_f) { return ""; }
    if tf_f.path == "" { return "no font has been loaded"; }
    if !tf_f.path.starts_with("/") {
        return "`{tf_f.path}` is a RELATIVE font path — it would mean the process cwd on `graphics` 0.5.2 and the entry's directory on 0.8.0, so only an absolute one is accepted";
    }
    if !is_file(tf_f.path) {
        return "no font file at `{tf_f.path}` — `{TEXT_FONT_FILE}` is missing from the repo";
    }
    "`{tf_f.path}` exists but `graphics::gl_load_font` refused it — not a font this build can read"
}

// How wide `tw_s` draws, in pixels.  0 for a font that did not load.
//
// ⚠ It matches `graphics::draw_text`'s own `as integer + 1` rather
// than rounding independently, so a caller that reserves this many
// pixels reserves exactly what the ink will occupy.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn text_width(tw_f: const Font, tw_s: text, tw_size: float) -> integer {
    if !font_ready(tw_f) { return 0; }
    graphics::gl_measure_text(tw_f.handle, tw_s, tw_size) as integer + 1
}

// One line's height in pixels.  0 for a font that did not load.
//
// ⚠⚠ It returns 0 rather than what `graphics` answers, and that is
// the point: `gl_text_height(null, 16.0)` measured **20** — a plain
// non-zero — so the underlying call cannot be used to ask whether a
// font is there.  `text_fault` is that question.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn text_line_height(tlh_f: const Font, tlh_size: float) -> integer {
    if !font_ready(tlh_f) { return 0; }
    graphics::gl_text_height(tlh_f.handle, tlh_size)
}

// Draw `td_s` at `(td_x, td_y)`, the TOP-left of the line.  Answers
// whether anything was drawn.
//
// ⚠⚠ **This is the only place in dryopea that may call
// `graphics::draw_text`.**  A second call site is a second place the
// handle check can be forgotten, and § A NULL HANDLE DRAWS is what
// forgetting it costs — not a blank screen, but the wrong typeface
// with every gate still green.
//
// ⚠ The boolean is what makes the refusal VISIBLE to a test: a draw
// that silently does nothing and a draw that succeeded look the same
// from the outside, and `tests/b1_the_text_seam.loft` asserts on this
// as well as on the pixels.
//
// Example: @DRY-096
pub fn text_draw(td_cv: Canvas, td_f: const Font, td_s: text, td_size: float,
                 td_x: integer, td_y: integer, td_colour: integer) -> boolean {
    if !font_ready(td_f) { return false; }
    if td_s == "" { return false; }
    td_cv.draw_text(td_f.handle, td_s, td_size, td_x, td_y, td_colour);
    true
}
